 the Rust library SEPARATE files and\n  SEPARATE `FILE*` handles, then compare the resulting file CONTENTS byte-for-byte.\n- Cover: `LZ4F_writeOpen` with prefs NULL, each blockSizeID (default/4/5/6/7), an invalid\n  blockSizeID (e.g. 1,2,3,8 → expect `LZ4F_ERROR_maxBlockSize_invalid` = `err(2)`),\n  fp = NULL and handle = NULL (→ `err(21)` parameter_null);\n  `LZ4F_write` with size 0, < maxWriteSize, == maxWriteSize, > maxWriteSize (chunking),\n  buf = NULL, handle = NULL; non-default prefs forwarded (contentChecksum, blockChecksum,\n  contentSize, dictID, level 0/9/12, autoFlush 0/1, blockLinked vs blockIndependent);\n  `LZ4F_writeClose` normal, after a prior write error, and handle = NULL;\n  `LZ4F_readOpen` on a file shorter than 19 bytes (→ `err(23)` io_read), on frames with each\n  blockSizeID, on a frame with an invalid blockSizeID byte (→ `err(2)`), fp/handle NULL;\n  `LZ4F_read` with size 0, less than remaining content, more than remaining content (short\n  count at EOF), several sequential reads, linked vs independent blocks, with checksums,\n  and buf/handle NULL; `LZ4F_readClose` normal and NULL.\n- PROPERTY-STYLE: for the happy paths, loop over MANY randomized payloads (fixed seed) and\n  randomized write/read chunk-size sequences, asserting every return value matches and the\n  round-tripped data equals the original for both libraries, and that the compressed FILE\n  BYTES produced by C and Rust are identical.\n- Also cross-check: read a C-written file with the RUST reader and a Rust-written file with\n  the C reader.\n\nIterate on both files: run each with `--offline --release`; fix YOUR TEST when the test\nitself is at fault. Only genuine C-vs-Rust behavioural differences should remain failing.\n\nFinish by reporting, per file: test count, pass/fail counts, and a precise list of any\ngenuine C-vs-Rust divergences.","agentId":"a48e6a99212014167","agentType":"general-purpose","harnessNoteCount":0,"harnessTailCount":0,"harnessSectionHash":"6cef4695b8ebbf62","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both files are written and fully passing. No `c_src/` or `translation/src/` file was touched.\n\n## `translation/tests/xxhash.rs` — 22 tests, 22 pass, 0 fail\n\nCoverage: one `row_NN_*` test per CONFIGS row 163–173 (plus two extra `row_163_164_165_166_*` / `row_171_*` exhaustive sweeps), and `errors_1NN_*` tests for ERRORS.md rows 185–195.\n\n- Exhaustive length sweep `0..=300` for both `LZ4_XXH32` and `LZ4_XXH64` (all `len&15` / `len&31` residues) × 4 shapes × seeds `{0, 1, 0x9E3779B1, 0xFFFFFFFF, 0x12345678}` (XXH64 also `0x9E377 9B185EBCA87`, `u64::MAX`, random 64-bit), plus 1 KB / 64 KB / 1 MB inputs.\n- Misaligned inputs: slices at offsets 0..7 of a malloc-aligned buffer, lengths 0..200 plus 1 KB / 4 KB.\n- Streaming: `createState`/`reset`/`update`/`digest`/`freeState`/`freeState(NULL)`/`copyState`; single-update == one-shot; ~20 chunk patterns per length (1-byte-at-a-time, sub-stripe sums, zero-length updates, exact/straddling 16- and 32-byte stripe boundaries, 6 random splits) with C-vs-Rust digest compared *after every chunk* and cross-checked against each library's one-shot of the consumed prefix; digest twice; digest→update→digest; `copyState` + diverging updates (copies only ever between same-library states).\n- Canonical: 506 values each for 32/64-bit, comparing the canonical **bytes** (and asserting big-endian), plus `hashFromCanonical` on 200 arbitrary byte patterns.\n- Error paths assert the exact `XXH_errorcode`: `update(state, NULL, len)` → `XXH_ERROR (1)` for len 0/1/15/16/17/1000 (and that the state is unmodified), `reset` → `XXH_OK (0)` for all seeds, `freeState(NULL)` → `XXH_OK`, one-shot `(NULL, 0, seed)` == one-shot of an empty buffer. `LZ4_XXH_versionNumber` == 605.\n\n## `translation/tests/lz4file.rs` — 19 tests, 19 pass, 0 fail\n\nOne `row_NN_*` test per CONFIGS row 157–162 plus `errors_*` tests. C and Rust always get separate scratch files (`std::env::temp_dir()`, unique names, RAII cleanup — verified no leftovers), separate `FILE*`s from the process libc (`fopen/fclose/fflush/fseek/ftell/fread/fwrite/setvbuf/fmemopen`), separate handles; compressed file bytes are compared byte-for-byte.\n\n- `writeOpen`: prefs NULL, all valid blockSizeIDs, invalid IDs `{1,2,3,8,9,100,u32::MAX}` → `err(2)`, `fp=NULL`/`handle=NULL`/both → `err(21)` (handle left/set NULL verified).\n- `write`: size 0, 1, 17, `MWS-1`, `MWS`, `MWS+1`, `2·MWS`, `5·MWS` (chunk loop), multi-write mixes, `buf=NULL` (size 0 and non-zero) and `handle=NULL` → `err(21)`.\n- prefs forwarding: 4 checksum combos, both blockModes, levels 0/1/9/10/12, autoFlush 0/1, dictIDs, exact contentSize, an all-non-default combo, plus a wrong declared `contentSize` → `writeClose` = `err(14)`.\n- `writeClose`: normal, empty frame (exactly 4 trailer bytes), `NULL` → `err(21)`, after a latched write error (via `/dev/full`) → `0`, short trailer write (via a size-limited `fmemopen`) → `err(22)`; `writeOpen` short header write → `err(22)`.\n- `readOpen`: files of 0/1/4/6/7/11/18 bytes → `err(23)`; all blockSizeIDs; hand-crafted headers → `err(13)`, `err(2)` (blockSizeID bytes 0–3), `err(8)` ×3, `err(6)`, `err(17)`; skippable frame; NULL params → `err(21)`.\n- `read`/`readClose`: size 0, partial, over-long (short count at EOF), 6 sequential-size schedules, linked vs independent × both checksums × 2 block sizes, corrupt and truncated frames (error forwarded verbatim), NULL buf/handle → `err(21)`, `readClose(NULL)` → `err(21)`, stream position at EOF via `ftell`.\n- Property test: 40 randomized iterations (fixed seed) over shape/length/prefs/write-chunking/read-chunking, plus cross-reads (C-written file read by Rust and vice versa) in **every** happy-path round-trip.\n\n## C-vs-Rust divergences\n\n**None.** Every return value, every hash, and every compressed byte agreed. Three real C behaviours that my first draft got wrong (now pinned as assertions, both libraries matching):\n\n1. `LZ4F_writeClose` returns the `LZ4F_compressEnd` **byte count** on success, not `0` (lz4file.c:326-340); only `err(22)`/a forwarded error means failure.\n2. `LZ4F_readOpen` unconditionally demands 19 bytes, so a frame shorter than that — e.g. the 11-byte header+endMark frame produced by an empty payload — can never be reopened: `err(23) io_read`.\n3. ERRORS row 184 confirmed: after `LZ4F_write` latches `err(22)`, `LZ4F_writeClose` skips `compressEnd` and returns `LZ4F_OK_NoError (0)`, silently dropping the earlier error.\n\nNot forceable without an allocator hook (documented in comments, not tested): ERRORS rows 161, 162, 166, 172, 174, 175, 176, 189, 190 (allocation / inner-context failures), row 168 (`fread` \"negative\" branch, unreachable since `ret` is `size_t` — the reachable EOF short-count consequence *is* tested), rows 192/194 NULL-state UB, and rows 195–198 (compile-time asserts)."}],"resolvedModel":"global.anthropic.claude-opus-5[1m]","totalDurationMs":952348,"totalTokens":204307,"totalToolUseCount":43,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2439,"cache_read_input_tokens":199614,"output_tokens":2252,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":2439},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":9,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":26,"editFileCount":8,"linesAdded":2746,"linesRemoved":33,"otherToolCount":0}}}
