{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a3448a90c8ffceca8","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01SPboieZ5XyrPV9wqSZrmi4","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-glo4ZM/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-iXd6IA/e6275ffd-eea5-4c85-9a55-8638b42a0b62/tasks/a3448a90c8ffceca8.output","summary":"Done.\n\n## Deliverable\n\n`/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-iXd6IA/translation/tests/lz4_stream.rs` — 3705 lines, one new file. Nothing under `c_src/` or `translation/src/` was touched (verified with `find -newermt`).\n\n## Results\n\n- **32 tests, 32 pass, 0 fail.**\n- Runtime: **6.7 s** wall (parallel), **78 s** single-threaded — both well inside the 150 s budget.\n- Full crate suite still green (`lz4_block.rs` 34 pass, `lz4file.rs`, `smoke.rs`).\n- Coverage audit: `row_35_…` … `row_65_…` all present (one test per CONFIGS row 35-65), plus `fuzz_rows_54_62_corrupted_and_truncated_chains`.\n\n## Genuine C-vs-Rust divergences found\n\n**None.** Every C/Rust pair agreed on return value, payload bytes, and full destination-buffer contents (including scratch bytes written past the reported output but inside `dstCapacity`) across every configuration exercised.\n\n## Notes on the approach\n\n- Each library creates/frees its own `LZ4_stream_t`/`LZ4_streamDecode_t` and its own `LZ4_sizeofStreamState()` caller buffers; no opaque state ever crosses libraries. Input buffers are shared (identical pointers), destination buffers are separate and pre-filled with `0xCD` plus a 64-byte guard that is asserted intact.\n- Cross-decompression is wired in structurally: the C-produced block chain is always fed to the *Rust* decoder and vice versa.\n- `LZ4_renormDictT` (row 49) is reached *for real*, not approximated: `LZ4_loadDict(stream, dict, 3)` returns early leaving `tableType == clearedTable` and `currentOffset == 64 KB`, so ~32 766 cheap `LZ4_resetStream_fast` calls walk `currentOffset` to `0x7FFE0000`/`0x7FFF0000`/`0x80000000` in microseconds. Three variants then trigger renorm on the first block, mid-chain with a populated hash table, and with `dictSize > 64 KB` (clamp path). A 400-block/3 MB run covers the \"long chain\" axis.\n- Negative control performed: perturbing the comparison helper by one made `row_35` fail immediately; resolved symbol addresses for C vs Rust were confirmed distinct, so the suite is not silently comparing one library with itself.\n\nThree failures encountered during development were **test bugs**, not translation bugs, and were fixed:\n1. `row_44` — my encoder ring buffer let a block *fully* cover the previous block, which clobbers the encoder's dictionary without the `sourceEnd < dictEnd` overlap recompute firing (outside the documented ring-buffer contract). Fixed by capping block size at `ring_size / 2`.\n2. `row_58` — rotating-buffer test filled all blocks before compressing, so later blocks overwrote earlier regions. Fixed by filling and compressing in the same step.\n3. `row_61` — asserted that a single-byte corruption must fail; it can legitimately decode. Restricted `row_61` to guaranteed failures (too-small `dstCapacity`, truncated block, which must leave the prefix state intact — proven by re-decoding the same block correctly) and moved random corruption to the fuzz test, which drives both decoders through the whole damaged chain and compares exact return values step by step.","usage":{"total_tokens":225179,"tool_uses":50,"duration_ms":2475598},"uuid":"448a09a6-5df4-492e-a477-e54fdeb0f4bc","session_id":"e6275ffd-eea5-4c85-9a55-8638b42a0b62"}
