{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a555565b18efcb1ef","tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01FiEyveHJh3Q3MJJNHTCWT9","status":"completed","output_file":"/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-agent-glo4ZM/tmp/claude-31072124/-local-home-scheschb--harvest-work-harvest-work-iXd6IA/e6275ffd-eea5-4c85-9a55-8638b42a0b62/tasks/a555565b18efcb1ef.output","summary":"Done. All 31 tests pass; no C-vs-Rust divergence found.\n\n## What was written\n\n**One new file: `/local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-iXd6IA/translation/tests/errors_frame.rs`** (3159 lines). Nothing else was touched — `c_src/` and `translation/src/` are unmodified (verified byte-identical file set; full suite still green: 31/34/32/19/38/38/3/22 all pass).\n\n## Results\n\n`cd translation && cargo test --offline --release --test errors_frame` → **31 tests, 31 passed, 0 failed** (~1.6 s).\n\n- 23 row tests, `err_1_…` through `err_23_…` (every number 1..=23 appears in a test fn name; verified by script).\n- 8 FFI-boundary tests: `ffi_null_pointers_into_every_compression_entry_point`, `ffi_zero_and_one_byte_dstCapacity_and_zero_srcSize`, `ffi_out_of_range_enum_values_through_compressFrame`, `ffi_out_of_range_enum_values_through_lowlevel_pipeline`, `ffi_nonzero_reserved_fields_are_ignored_identically`, `ffi_context_version_is_stored_but_never_validated`, `ffi_isError_getErrorCode_getErrorName_boundary`, `ffi_property_random_parameter_and_capacity_sweep` (500 randomized iterations, fixed seed).\n\nNon-vacuity was confirmed by a negative control (temporarily asserting `compressEnd == 5` instead of `4` produced a clean failure; reverted, file byte-identical to the committed version).\n\n## Rows forced for real via the failing-allocator technique\n\nAn `LZ4F_CustomMem` with `extern \"C\"` alloc/calloc/free shims (fail unconditionally, or on the Nth call, with the counter carried through `opaqueState` so the shims stay reentrant); `customCalloc` is exercised both present and absent (the latter drives the `customAlloc + memset` branch). The free-hook also proves zero leaks in every case.\n\n| row | site | how |\n|---|---|---|\n| 3 | inner error forwarded out of `LZ4F_compressFrame_usingCDict` | `fail_at`=2/3 → `err(9)` surfaces through `FORWARD_IF_ERROR` |\n| 4 | `LZ4F_createCDict_advanced` cdict alloc | `fail_at`=1 and `-1` → NULL, exactly 1 alloc attempted |\n| 5 | `dictContent`/`fastCtx`/`HCCtx` | `fail_at` swept 1..=8 × 5 dict sizes → NULL for 1..4, valid for 5..8, all 4 sites attempted, partial cdict freed |\n| 6 | `LZ4F_createCompressionContext_advanced` calloc | `fail_at` swept, proves exactly 1 alloc site |\n| 10 | `LZ4_stream_t`/`LZ4_streamHC_t` in `compressBegin_internal` | `fail_at`=2 across 7 compression levels → `err(9)` |\n| 11 | `cctx->tmpBuff` | `fail_at` swept 1..=8 × 4 pref sets → `err(9)` for 1..3, success for ≥4 |\n| 21 | `LZ4F_createDecompressionContext_advanced` calloc | `fail_at` swept × 5 `version` values → NULL |\n\n## Rows that remain unforceable (kept as tests, documented in-file)\n\n- **Row 8** (`LZ4F_createCompressionContext` → `err(9)`) and **row 23** (`LZ4F_createDecompressionContext` → `err(9)`): these entry points hard-code `LZ4F_defaultCMem` (lz4frame.c:624, :1306), so the allocation is libc `calloc` of a few-hundred-byte object. There is no hook; forcing it needs process-wide `calloc` interposition, which would also break the Rust harness's own allocator. Asserted instead: the *branch condition* (advanced ctor returning NULL under an injected failure) is identical in both libraries, the normal path returns `LZ4F_OK_NoError` + non-NULL in both, and `LZ4F_getErrorName(err(9)) == \"ERROR_allocation_failed\"` in both.\n- **Row 16** (`LZ4F_flush` → `err(20)`): logically unreachable, not an allocator problem. `cStage` only becomes 0 at lz4frame.c:1233 inside `LZ4F_compressEnd`, which first calls `LZ4F_flush` (line 1213) and that sets `tmpInSize = 0` (line 1185); the only writer of `tmpInSize` (line 1097) is itself gated on `cStage == 1` (line 1005). So `cStage == 0` ⇒ `tmpInSize == 0`. Asserted instead: on a fresh and on a just-ended cctx, `LZ4F_flush` returns **0** (the `tmpInSize == 0` early-out at line 1167 wins over the `cStage` test) — not `err(20)` — identically in both libraries, across 7 capacities.\n\n## Genuine C-vs-Rust divergences\n\n**None.** Every return value, every error sentinel, and every destination buffer (compared in full, including the 0xCD padding outside the reported output) matched.\n\n## C hazards found while building the envelope (not divergences — both libraries behave identically where observable)\n\nRecorded as comments in the test file; the tests deliberately stay inside the safe envelope so as not to trip C's own UB:\n\n1. `LZ4F_flush`'s capacity check (lz4frame.c:1169) budgets only `tmpInSize + BHSize + BFSize`, while `LZ4F_makeBlock` returns `BHSize + cSize + crcFlag * BFSize` (line 907). An out-of-contract `blockChecksumFlag` of `0xFFFFFFFF` makes the *return value* overshoot the buffer by ~16 GB (and `blockChecksumFlag == 2` overruns the given capacity by 4 bytes for a stored block). `drive_pipeline` therefore stops the sequence when a call reports writing more than the capacity it was given — the return value itself is still compared, and it matches.\n2. An out-of-range `blockSizeID` makes `maxBlockSize == (size_t)-2` while `requiredBuffSize = maxBlockSize + 128 KB` wraps to only **131070** bytes (lz4frame.c:742-751), so a multi-chunk `autoFlush == 0` session would make the C overrun its own `tmpBuff` at line 1096. Out-of-range ids are therefore swept exhaustively with a single small (4 KB) update, and the randomized property test keeps `blockSizeID ∈ {0,4,5,6,7}`.\n3. `LZ4F_compressUpdateImpl` line 1014 assigns `LZ4F_flush(...)` to `bytesWritten` and adds it to `dstPtr` with **no `FORWARD_IF_ERROR`** — an error there becomes a wild pointer. Reachable only via a `blockCompressMode` switch with a capacity that passes lines 1006/1009 but fails line 1169; not exercised (pure UB).","usage":{"total_tokens":247949,"tool_uses":49,"duration_ms":1289254},"uuid":"6757c70d-7be4-4401-afee-309ff3622127","session_id":"e6275ffd-eea5-4c85-9a55-8638b42a0b62"}
